{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) allowable annual rent increase","requirement":"Rent on RSO-covered units may be raised only once every 12 months, by no more than the annual allowable percentage published by LAHD; since February 2, 2026, landlords may not add a utilities percentage or the extra 10% for an added dependent, though a 10% increase for an additional non-dependent tenant remains allowed. Rent may reset to market rate after certain vacancies, such as a voluntary move-out, an eviction for nonpayment or lease violation, or a Tenant Buyout Agreement, and further increases require LAHD approval through the capital improvement, renovation, seismic retrofit, rehabilitation or just-and-reasonable programs.","key_value":"One increase per 12 months, capped at the LAHD annual allowable percentage (3% for July 1, 2025 - June 30, 2026); no added utility percentage from 2026-02-02; RSO surcharge $1.61/month and SCEP surcharge $2.83/month may be passed through separately with written notice","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance, LAMC § 151.00 et seq.","coverage_conditions":"Rental properties first built on or before October 1, 1978, plus replacement units under LAMC § 151.28. Covered types include apartments, condominiums, townhomes, duplexes, two or more single-family dwellings on one parcel, hotel/motel/rooming-house/boarding-house rooms occupied by the same tenant for more than 30 consecutive days, residential units attached to commercial buildings, ADUs, JADUs, and mobile homes and RVs in mobile home parks.","exemptions":"Rent amount is not regulated for condominium and townhome tenancies that began after December 31, 1995. Landlords may apply for a Luxury Exemption based on the monthly rent charged on or before May 31, 1978. Temporary exemptions are mentioned but not detailed.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[{"id":"c3","reason":"The source only mentions the RSO in the context of eviction filings (the Landlord Declaration requirement). It states no rent increase cap, coverage or exemption, so it is not a rent_increase_limits rule."}]}
